  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">FemFocus - Oxfam Novib-Combatting Violence Against Women &amp; Supporting Women HR Defenders in MENA: Egypt, Iraq, Jordan, Lebanon, Syria, OPT and Tunisia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Violence against women (VAW) remains widespread across MENA; one in three women faces physical, sexual or psychological violence rooted in discriminatory laws and gender norms, with differing patterns across the seven proposed implementation countries (Egypt, Iraq, Jordan, Lebanon, Occupied Palestinian Territories, Syria and Tunisia). Multiple drivers intersect: conflict, displacement and economic crises weaken institutions and protection mechanisms. Although all have ratified CEDAW, implementation is limited due to legal gaps and unclear domestic-violence provisions. Patriarchal norms also sustain economic violence by restricting women&#x2019;s access to income, assets and financial decision-making.

Oxfam Novib will function as the support partner by providing financial support and assist in-country partners in strengthening their capacity for the implementation of activities aimed at combating violence against women and supporting women human rights defenders. In-country partners are responsible for the design and execution of these activities, with the support partner facilitating this process through a transparent application, selection, and funding mechanism, as well as providing oversight and supervision.

Building on Oxfam Novib&apos;s research, experience, long-standing regional presence, and in-country partner (ICP) networks, this programme will fund locally led interventions across four result areas:
1) Prevention of VAW including information/ education services, digital and citizen engagement (including men and boys) for social norms change.
2) Survivor-centred services including safe spaces, (mobile) psychosocial and SRH care, socio-economic reintegration, information and legal support.
3) Legal, policy and practice change including evidence-generation, campaigning, policy dialogue and coalition-building.
4) WHRD support including urgent and long-term legal support, wellbeing, temporary re-location, digital resilience and safe spaces.

Flexible, size-differentiated grants will enable in-country partners (ICPs) to combine activities across one or multiple result areas, depending on context and expertise. The programme will offer the following types of grants:
&#x2022; Impact grants: for established ICPs implementing comprehensive and complex programmes addressing up to four result areas;
&#x2022; Catalyst grants: for ICPs with a specific thematic or geographical focus;
&#x2022; Defenders&#x2019; grants: for ICPs dedicated to supporting women human rights defenders.</narrative>
  </description>
